---
description: "Self-review checklist — run through before opening a PR or asking for review"
alwaysApply: false
---

Before opening a PR, go through this explicitly (don't just say "looks good"):

- Does the change match the plan agreed on, or did scope drift during implementation?
- Did tests actually run (not just written) — unit, integration, and full suite if the change touches shared code?
- Any security items relevant to this change (see security-standards) — checked, not skipped?
- Any other feature/flow that could break because of this change — checked the callers/consumers of anything touched?
- Any leftover debug code, commented-out blocks, console logs, or TODOs that shouldn't ship?
- Does the diff match the commit messages — no unrelated changes bundled in?
- Is the PR description accurate: what changed, why, how it was tested, anything the human reviewer should pay special attention to?

If any answer is "not sure," that's the signal to check before opening the PR, not after.
